.TH SHEDIT 1 "2026-11-16" "shedit 1.0" "Terminal text input"

.SH NAME
shedit \- terminal text input

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shedit
.RB [ \-bhx ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR height ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR text ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shedit
is a terminal text input program.
Unlike an editor it is not using the whole terminal.
It opens at the cursor position and waits for text input.

.PP
On submit the whole text is printed to stdout.

.PP
On start a text can be preloaded from stdin.
See examples.

.PP
.B shedit
is meant to be a simple input program.
There is only minimal editing with backspace and cursor keys.
But it is possible to call your editor from
.BR $EDITOR .

.PP
.B shedit
is part of the
.B shui-tools
collection.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short usage message.

.TP
.BI \-r " lines"
Defines the height of the input field.
A value of
.B 0
uses the whole terminal.
The default is
.BR 3 .

.TP
.B \-x
By default,
.B Ctrl-D
or
.B Esc d
are the submit keys.
With
.BR \-x ,
the
.B Enter
key becomes a submit key.

.TP
.B \-b
Display the status bar in reverse video.

.TP
.BI \-p " text"
Set the status bar text.

.TP
.B \-o
Turns shedit into a one line input. Enter is submit and all keys for editor or newline are disabled.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B Ctrl-J
Insert a newline.

.TP
.B Ctrl-E
or
.B Esc e
Open the editor specified in
.BR $EDITOR .

.TP
.B Ctrl-X
or
.B Esc
or
.B Esc q
or
.B Esc x
Abort without saving the text and exit with return code 1.

.TP
.B Ctrl-D
or
.B Esc d
Submit the text and exit with return code 0.

.SH EXAMPLES
Wait for a message to be typed:

.PP
.nf
chat=$(shedit)
.fi

.PP
Preload
.I intro.txt
with a height of 10 lines:

.PP
.nf
chat=$(shedit -r10 < intro.txt)
.fi

.PP
Edit a stream before saving:

.PP
.nf
jq '.[]' file.json | shedit -r20 > new.json
.fi

.PP
Edit a filename

.PP
.nf
new_filename=$(printf "${filename}" | shedit -o)
.fi

